::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify x^4 * x^3. {
=x^7 #Correct. The bases match, so add the exponents: 4+3=7.
~x^12 #Not quite. This multiplies the exponents instead of adding them.
~2x^7 #Not quite. The base x is not added to another x.
~x^(4/3) #Not quite. Multiplication of powers with the same base uses addition of exponents.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify m^8 / m^2. {
~m^10 #Not quite. This adds exponents, but division with the same base uses subtraction.
=m^6 #Correct. The bases match, so subtract the exponents: 8-2=6.
~m^4 #Not quite. This divides the exponents instead of subtracting them.
~1 #Not quite. The exponents are not equal, so the quotient is not 1.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (b^3)^4. {
~b^7 #Not quite. This adds exponents, but a power raised to a power uses multiplication.
~b^1 #Not quite. This subtracts exponents, but the operation is not division.
=b^12 #Correct. A power raised to another power means multiply the exponents: 3*4=12.
~4b^3 #Not quite. The outside exponent is not a coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Simplify (2x^2)^3. {
~2x^6 #Not quite. The outside exponent must also apply to the coefficient 2.
~6x^6 #Not quite. 2^3 is 8, not 6.
~8x^5 #Not quite. The exponent on x should be 2*3=6.
=8x^6 #Correct. Apply the exponent to each factor: 2^3=8 and (x^2)^3=x^6.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
[MA.912.NSO.1.2] Which expression is equivalent to p^2 * q^2? {
=p^2q^2 #Correct. The bases are different, so the exponents do not combine.
~pq^4 #Not quite. The exponents cannot be added because the bases are different.
~p^4q #Not quite. The exponent on q was lost.
~(pq)^4 #Not quite. This changes the value by raising both variables to the fourth power.
}
